ddresses all  right so i'm gonna tell the full story  behind  when and where i've personally gotten  raises  and then we're going to circle back at  the end and i'm going to talk about  why i think those happen and some key  takeaways  and i'm going to keep the focus here on  the percentage increases that i've  received  because there's so much variation  globally and regionally in salaries  and at the end of the day the most  important thing is no matter where  you're starting from  that over time you continue to move up  so when i got my first job fresh out of  my master's degree  i had a base salary but i had to wait a  solid year  before i got my first raise and i know  this makes for a pretty long  first year but i think this is pretty  standard with most organizations  when i got my first ever raise it had  been about a year and three months or so  since i'd started  it was time for the company to do annual  compensation adjustments  and so that first raise amounted to five  percent  not bad covers the loss of the  purchasing power of your money to  inflation  all that good stuff unfortunately though  with that organization  another year came around and i'd  received a promotion and all that jazz  but due to a variety of external  circumstances  the company was not able to perform more  compensation adjustments  so somewhere between the two and a half  and three year mark  i made the decision to leave the company  and trust me when i say  that was not an easy decision because i  absolutely  loved the people there and the work i  was doing and i had learned a lot during  my time there  in my case there were some personal  reasons for it too  but yes the money thing was definitely a  significant factor in my decision  now when i was leaving companies i asked  my new organization for an adjustment of  about  25 upwards to be totally fair  that was an incredibly tall ask and no  they did not grant that  they ended up coming back to me with an  offer to give me a 15  adjustment and we ended up negotiating  and meeting in the middle with a 19  adjustment that brought me through some  work that i was doing on a longer term  contract basis with a specific sponsor  and that work lasted about 18 months at  almost exactly the one year mark along  the way  i was given a bump of about 5.5 percent  now just to give some context on a  timeline here  this was shortly after i'd started my  youtube channel it was brand new  i was just getting it off the ground and  i am going to circle back to that  because that's going to be relevant  later  so a few months later that contract work  came to a close  and i came back to a full-time position  within my hiring organization  that came with a promotion shortly  thereafter  and that promotion came with an 11.5  percent raise or so  and then just recently it came time to  do a second round of annual compensation  adjustments  which for me came out to about an  additional nine percent  so it's been now almost five years since  i finished my master's degree  and entered the professional world full  time  excluding various consulting gigs that i  was doing pretty consistently throughout  grad school  and since then i've been fortunate  enough to receive what i think  pretty much anybody would consider to be  generous which is about a  60 percent movement upward in salary  now in fairness if we were trying to  represent how much i know  now and how much value i can provide to  organizations  as opposed to five years ago the  difference between them would be many  times greater than 60 percent  but that's not the point and it's  impossible to create a fair true  compensation structure based on that  and so i'm now going to provide five  insights into how exactly  i think i was able to move up so fast  the first point is that moving up in  salary  starts before you even have your first  full-time job with a salary  it starts all the way back in college  let's think about this  all the way back with the economic  reason why you get paid a salary in the  first place  and essentially it's because you provide  surplus  economic value to an organization i get  that that's a little bit simplistic  in many cases but the philosophical  underpinnings of that are  true the more value that you're able to  provide an  organization the better i recommend  watching my video  all about how to break into data science  to best  set yourself up because really  everything there applies to this  discussion about salary  i think the most important point in this  salary discussion is  if you're coming from previous work  experience whether that's an internship  or even unpaid work if it has to be that  that's going to go a long way when i was  in grad school i did paid teaching  an unpaid research gig for some  connections that i knew who needed  some help a paid internship  working as a consultant also and then a  separate  unpaid consulting gig for an outside  organization  and except for the teaching job i landed  every single  one of those jobs just through my  network  because either i knew people who needed  help  or those people who i knew knew somebody  else who needed help  and i think having some of that prior  experience under my belt  really helped me get into my first job  at at least a decent starting point or  more than it would have been otherwise  now the second takeaway here is that  you're not necessarily guaranteed to get  a raise  if you don't ask for one when you do  decide to ask for a raise  there's one of a couple different ways  that you should try to frame it  one approach is that you may be  underpaid relative to the general market  so for example it could be data  scientists in your region  with the same level of experience  typically make 100k on average  you find that out through doing your own  research but maybe you only make  75k that may be a mistake on your  company's part  and that may be grounds for an  adjustment then the other approach  is very clearly with numbers  demonstrating that you provide  way more value to the organization than  you're taking  out from them in the form of  compensation but of course be reasonable  about  it i mean don't ask for a 30 movement  upward while your company is  restructuring and going through layoffs  or something like that  but you never know ask and you just  might receive  sometimes it doesn't work out though and  that's going to bring me to my next  point  which is that yeah sometimes it is  appropriate to switch jobs  so there's a bit of a balancing act to  this one because if you go and you  switch  jobs every year or every year and a half  it is going to look like a major red  flag after a while  and it's going to make companies start  to get a little hesitant to invest in  you  having said that it's well known that  changing jobs will typically give you  some of the biggest boosts in salary  that you're ever going to get  so this is from an article from  marketwatch and they've got a cpa and a  tax expert who stated that  consistently remaining employed at  companies for longer than two years  can decrease your lifetime earnings by  as much as fifty percent  but then it's well known that annual  raises are typically around three  percent or so  but then he estimates changing jobs  whenever you do it  will get you a 10 to 20 percent increase  at the end of the day there's a lot more  that goes into deciding do you stay or  do you go  than just the cash alone but just know  what you're signing up for  if you're intending to stay at a company  for the long haul  now the next point here is when you're  actually getting hired in  negotiate and ask for more money right  then  because there's no better time to do so  so there's a little bit of a game that  typically gets played between hr  professionals and  job seekers in the sense that when they  give you their first  offer letter they're typically gonna  lowball you a little bit  and if you think about it you can't  really blame them  because what happens is so many people  once they receive a job offer  they're going to counter offer that well  if you suppose  a certain percentage of people don't  counter offer  well the company is able to go a little  bit lower with them  and the company has just saved  themselves a considerable amount of  money  don't be part of that percentage that  doesn't ask for more  now obviously be polite and be  reasonable and measured about it  ask them if there's some wiggle room for  some adjustment on your salary  come prepared with some statistics and  some market research  on how much data scientists in your area  with your level of experience typically  make  and if you're not a jerk about it you're  not gonna lose your original  offer trust me and you might just set  yourself up with more money then  which is gonna get compounded years in  the future in the form of raises  so your more money gets turned into more  more money  then the last takeaway here is probably  the most important one of all of them  and it's to build a network and a real  public presence  as i stated earlier in this video  literally  almost every single professional  opportunity i've  ever come upon came because i knew  somebody through my network  not because i blew people away with some  amazing project or portfolio or  something  so networking can get you in the door  and it can open up  other doors in the future once you get  some experience under your belt  you can take this a step further and so  this is partly speculation on my part  i don't know this to a hundred percent  certainty  but i don't think i would have done as  well for myself in the last couple years  in the money department  if i didn't have this youtube channel  where i contribute to things in the body  of data science knowledge out there  because i feel i have something to add  to it  people can look at it this way okay this  guy has  over 5000 subscribers located all around  the globe  more than 5000 people care what he has  to say i guess he must be legit  now i'm not saying any of this to put  myself on a pedestal  at all because i think way more people  can contribute in public forums like  youtube  and don't even realize it there are  plenty of options out there other than  just  youtube you can start writing blog  articles on medium  you can become a regular contributor at  places like github or stack overflow  or you could even build up your network  on linkedin and post there regularly  there's tons of things that you can do  to put yourself out there  you just need to figure out what makes  the most sense for you  i will end this one with a word of  caution though  be careful and don't do anything that'll  get you in trouble  there's a well-known youtuber tech lead  who got fired from facebook  for his youtube channel so when in doubt  talk to your boss  over communicate so that's my story  along with  what i think has stood out and been  successful for me  another word of caution that i'll try to  leave you with here is  just be careful as you chase higher  salaries  there's a lot of other potentially  competing interests  to pay attention to as well things like  benefits paid time off  work life balance in general just me  personally  i know a lot of really miserable people  who make a whole lot of money  but if you're in data science there's  plenty of ways out there to make money  and if you're not in data science try  some of these things anyway  i think they'll help you so thanks for  watching this video  if you enjoyed it please consider  sharing it hit the like button for the  youtube algorithm  and then leave me a comment down below  let me know how you made more money  professionally yourself  then i'll see you all in the not so  distant future until then  richard on data

